<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duleep Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Maharaja Duleep Singh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duleep Singh was the last maharaja of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, installed as a child after the violent succession struggles that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s death. After the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> wars the British annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1849, and he was removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, where he spent most of his life in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duleep-singh.jpg" alt="Duleep Singh" width="275" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Capt. Goldingham of London. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharajah_Duleep_Singh_dressed_for_a_State_function,_c._1875.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Duleep Singh was born on 6 September 1838, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> and the only child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jind-kaur">Maharani Jind Kaur</a>. After Ranjit Singh died in 1839, he lived quietly with his mother at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>. In 1843, after the assassinations of Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-singh">Sher Singh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhian-singh">Dhian Singh</a>, they were recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and on 16 September the Panch, the military council, proclaimed the five-year-old Maharaja with Jind Kaur as regent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The British East India Company declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> on 13 December 1845. After winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> it kept the boy as nominal ruler but replaced the Maharani with a Council of Regency and later imprisoned and exiled her. Thirteen years passed before he was allowed to see her again. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-sikh-war">Second Anglo-Sikh War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> was annexed on 29 March 1849 and he was deposed at the age of ten. He was given a pension of 120,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> a year and told he could live anywhere but the Punjab. His property, worth 15 million rupees, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a> diamond, was taken from him.</p><p>He was placed in the care of Dr John Login and sent to Fatehgarh, with tight limits on whom he could meet. Company policy was to Anglicise him in every respect. On 8 March 1853, before he turned fifteen, he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> with the approval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a>, who expected the conversion to destroy any remaining influence he had in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. The conversion remains controversial, and he later regretted it. In May 1854 he was sent into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In Britain Queen Victoria befriended him and was godmother to several of his children. He lived at Castle Menzies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a> and later bought the 17,000-acre Elveden estate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, which he turned into a game preserve. In 1861 he was allowed to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to meet his mother and bring her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and in 1863 he returned with her ashes. He married Bamba Müller in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 1864.</p><p>Disaffected, in debt and with his pension reduced, he reverted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a> and tried to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In 1886 he sailed for home, but the British stopped him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and made him return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which left him more bitter. He tried to find allies among other European powers and threatened an invasion of India through the North-West Frontier, but this was not taken seriously. Later he apologised to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and his pension was resumed. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and as the British feared unrest, his wish to be buried in India was refused; he lies in Elveden Church beside his wife Bamba. All his children died without legitimate issue.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duleep Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sher Singh was assassinated in 1843, along with the chief minister Dhian Singh, and the five-year-old <b>Duleep Singh</b> was installed under the regency of his mother, Maharani Jind Kaur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lahore Fort, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.59,74.31&amp;year=1873" aria-label="Lahore Fort on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6466 1013 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Duleep Singh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6916 1283h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6916 1283h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.59,74.31&amp;year=1873">What was here in 1873? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
